+    Each is an ordinary function in `Raylib` with raylib's signature, its kind's meta and `@:deprecated("message")`, its body a throw
+    that never runs. The compiler warns at every naming of a deprecated field, in a check it runs on every build anyway, and
+    `helix.ray.internal.Refusals` (an init macro in `extraParams.hxml`) reports each warning that is a refusal's message as an error
+    in `onGenerate`, at the same place: called, passed as a value, or in `$type(LoadTexture)`, the way agents probe signatures.
+    Every such naming in one build is reported, the call keeps raylib's result type, so nothing after it is typed wrong because of
+    it, and the message is in the source an agent reads. They were macros whose body returned `Build.unsupported(message, macro
+    (null : T))`; a macro can't be named without being called, so `$type(LoadTexture)` and `var f = LoadTexture` gave Haxe's
+    "Macro functions must be called immediately", never the message. Walking the typed program in `onAfterTyping` instead names
+    each one too, but the macro API's encoding of typed expressions doubled the build (+125% on a 193k-line program; 0.2 s cached
+    server builds became 2 s), where the deprecation check costs nothing measurable (+0.5%). What it gives up: the refusals are
+    reported once the program types, so another error hides them until it is fixed; an editor's diagnostics show them as
+    deprecation warnings, not errors; and code where deprecation warnings are off (its own `@:deprecated` field or class, or
+    `@:haxe.warning("-WDeprecated")`) isn't refused, the function throwing if called. A build with `-D no-deprecation-warnings` or
+    `-w -WDeprecated` is refused. A macro function refusing one form of its call (`LoadAutomationEventList` given a file) does it
+    through `Refusals.refuse`, reported the same way. Haxe's `#error` fires wherever the file is read, called or not, and
+    `@:require` refuses but leaves the call `Dynamic`, which errors again at its first use. In a section the function is marked
+    `@:unsupported("message")`, which `gen_raylib` writes into the facade, checking the signature the message names against the
+    call's own. `@:unsupported` is for what helix-ray won't have, by design (these loads,
+    rlgl's GL objects), and `@:unimplemented` for what it doesn't have yet, so an agent can tell a dead end from
     a gap. `gen_raylib` reads the sections as the compiler describes them (`haxe -xml`, and a macro for the facades' own macro
